WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.140
Let's go next to our caller from the three
zero eight area code.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.460
Who is calling today from three zero eight

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:10.150
Hey, can you hear me?

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:11.389
Yes I can.

00:00:11.389 --> 00:00:12.969
Who's this?

00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:15.360
This is Kowalski from Nebraska.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:16.360
Hey Kowalski.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:17.820
What's up?

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:26.710
Well, I just wanted to call in and say, uh,
I will actually, I was just kinda curious

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:32.349
last year the Midwest received like record
flooding and I was just curious how much that

00:00:32.349 --> 00:00:36.860
like hit the radar of people who live and
you know, the more popular areas of the country.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.380
That's a good question.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:42.530
I'm trying to think Carl recall story.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:48.609
I mean I remember hearing about flooding here
in, in from here in Boston, but it was certainly

00:00:48.609 --> 00:00:50.269
not like a major story.

00:00:50.269 --> 00:00:54.090
But you know, part of that may be I don't
really watch sort of like regular news where

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.850
you just get, you know, a breakdown of what's
going on in the news.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:04.720
I'm pretty narrowly consuming political news,
so it also may have been that just in my circles

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.590
it wasn't being talked about as much, but
I, I didn't hear that much about it.

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:11.070
I admit.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.070
Hmm.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:16.190
We did have like a dam failure and stuff and
unfortunately it's looking like we're not

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:19.910
going to have that great of a spring, so we
might be looking at some flooding again.

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:23.570
But I was just generally curious how well
that hit your radar.

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:29.360
And also, I recently talked to some people
from the Austin area working on some agricultural

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.050
software and stuff and they're pretty swell
people that admit you guys have some pretty

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.350
nice folks in the neighborhood.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:41.540
Yeah, I mean, is there the belief in Nebraska
that people in Boston are not nice?

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:44.500
Well, uh, I'm more

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.780
from the rural area of Nebraska, so I'm not
like Omaha or anything.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:53.820
I'm central Nebraska and there's kind of that
feeling of love, uh, the coasts or ignorance,

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:54.820
so to speak.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:59.530
But I got to say these people had really,
you know, just open minds and were very compassionate

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.010
and I generally liked them.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.010
That's fascinating.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:06.530
So there's a stereotype, rural central Nebraska.

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:07.760
Well, hold on a second.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.099
You're shot out of a cannon today.

00:02:09.099 --> 00:02:12.359
In, in, in central rural Nebraska.

00:02:12.359 --> 00:02:18.489
There is a stereotype that the people on the
coasts are the ignorant ones.

00:02:18.489 --> 00:02:20.599
I'm ignorant of agriculture.

00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:21.599
Oh, okay.

00:02:21.599 --> 00:02:22.599
Okay.

00:02:22.599 --> 00:02:23.599
Got it.

00:02:23.599 --> 00:02:24.599
Got it.

00:02:24.599 --> 00:02:28.049
Um, yeah, like, uh, there's this idea that
goes around that, and actually to be fair,

00:02:28.049 --> 00:02:33.430
they get admitted to this, to that, uh, many
generations removed from agriculture, don't

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:35.620
really understand like the processes and stuff.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:40.780
And I had to admit before going to like the
university, it was kind of an eye opener to

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:47.550
me how limited people's knowledge of like
agriculture is, but you know, you are your

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.690
environment, so it just makes sense.

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:51.690
Yeah.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:54.999
I grew up in Western Massachusetts where there
are a lot of farms, but they're, they're relatively

00:02:54.999 --> 00:02:55.999
small.

00:02:55.999 --> 00:02:59.519
It's not the large scale farming that you
see, uh, you know, in other parts of the country,

00:02:59.519 --> 00:03:02.319
including the Midwest and in the central California
Valley

00:03:02.319 --> 00:03:07.639
just has that gravitational pull of, you know,
with being just deep dominant and history

00:03:07.639 --> 00:03:08.639
and everything.

00:03:08.639 --> 00:03:10.830
So it just does overwhelm your whole life.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.430
But I'm not really an active listener of your
show.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:15.069
I watch a lot of it in passing.

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:18.780
Like, you know, some of the stuff you put
out, I think you do some good work and whatnot.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:19.780
So yeah.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:23.950
Let's just keep up the support there to the
folks in Iowa and make sure you give those

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:25.969
folks a call and I hope you have a good day.

00:03:25.969 --> 00:03:28.340
Hey and listen, how rural is your living situation?

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:32.079
Like how long of a ride is it just to go to
the grocery store?

00:03:32.079 --> 00:03:34.200
Um, for me it's not too bad.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:40.529
Um, I live outside of a, uh, it's actually
the largest city in Nebraska's third district,

00:03:40.529 --> 00:03:44.930
so I only live like a few miles away, but
I've got a lot of friends and we do a lot

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:47.159
of business and like some of the more rural
areas.

00:03:47.159 --> 00:03:54.349
And I got to say, um, okay, just, uh, so you
know, I am a registered Republican, although

00:03:54.349 --> 00:03:59.110
I would consider myself more of a radical
Republican, so to speak.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:03.459
I generally am like just somebody who thinks
that if you're going to live here, go with

00:04:03.459 --> 00:04:06.069
the dominant party and do your best to make
it moderate.

00:04:06.069 --> 00:04:07.980
Does that make sense?

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.749
I understand what you're saying.

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:10.769
I understand what you're saying.

00:04:10.769 --> 00:04:11.769
Yeah.

00:04:11.769 --> 00:04:14.310
But, so you were talking about how rural it
is.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:15.310
Yeah.

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:20.350
So like, uh, where I'm at isn't actually too
bad because I am close to a larger community

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:22.380
of like 55,000.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.000
But a lot of the areas I go to and where a
lot of my friends are from there, they do

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.620
not have those luxuries or like they have
to travel a couple of hours to go to the stores.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:36.850
You've got basically food deserts and uh,
the big problem we are facing is a medical

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:37.850
care.

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:38.850
Yeah.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:43.890
Like a problem is you can't really have a
heart attack in the middle of nowhere cause

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:48.620
even a helicopter will still take, you know,
two hours to get you where you need to be.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:49.690
So that is one thing.

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:55.030
Like I have been telling a lot of people around
here that, you know, Sanders plan for Medicare

00:04:55.030 --> 00:05:01.500
for all does include like just straight up
subsidies for rural communities to have clinics

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.950
to have full time nurses and whatnot.

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:09.340
And that would go a long way of also bringing
outside resources to help revitalize rural

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:10.340
America.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:14.300
So I keep telling everybody, you got to take
a pipe issue by issue basis.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:19.880
You know, so like when it comes to healthcare,
Sanders clearly blows everyone out of the

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:24.960
water, but, uh, you know, it just depends
on your priorities, but I would say,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:25.960
yeah.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:27.720
All right.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:31.900
Kuwalski you're starting to break up, so I'm
gonna let you go, but I appreciate hearing

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:32.900
from you and [inaudible]

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:37.180
for a super interesting to hear from someone
in relatively rural Nebraska.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:38.180
Let's take a quick break.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:42.280
If you're holding, don't hang up because I
will go right back to his bones after this

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:42.889
short range.

